{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-343884",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function  ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/ebcaf43d56e8854ce17d89ce66a811ad1f505f7ebb855362798d845164a80377.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
